      "name": "Pelagolux dominator",
      "note": "An aggressive deep-water phototroph combining Claude's proven nutricline-hunting strategy with energy-efficient buoyancy control and active predation. It uses nitrogen concentration as a depth gauge, floating when it reaches nutrient-rich water below approximately 1250 µm and sinking slowly when nutrients are less abundant. Its elongated rod body and substantial chloroplast provide enough photosynthetic income to sustain rapid growth. Starch and oil reserves support it through darkness, while emergency resting prevents starvation. Four lateral spines discourage attackers and interfere with feeding tubes. Two feeding peduncles extend in opposite directions, allowing it to drain competing species without needing to swim. It prioritises remaining near the nutrient-rich boundary, surviving the night and converting available resources into offspring as quickly as possible.",
